          <h2 class="cs-font_36 mb-4">Building off-site authority</h2>
          <p>Rankings this early are impressive — and authority is the lever that decides how much further they climb. For a brand starting from zero, credibility has to be built deliberately, so Apex Marketings worked the off-site side in parallel with the on-site program.</p>
          <p>That meant securing the <strong>GUSU partner link</strong> (a genuine, relevant industry relationship), listing Pak Gusu across <strong>GCC B2B directories and citations</strong>, and adding agency case-study backlinks. Google already counts links from sources including <strong>ensun.io, tendata, zoominfo and Yandex Maps</strong> — early signals that the entity is real and recognized.</p>
          <p>This is intentionally the ongoing chapter. Two months builds a credible foundation of authority; the compounding gains — pushing position #4 queries toward #1, and lifting the long tail — come from continuing to earn links and citations month over month.</p>

          <h2 class="cs-font_36 mb-4">Paid: Google Ads into a dedicated quote landing page</h2>
          <p>SEO and AEO build durable demand, but they take time to convert into clicks. Paid search fills that gap immediately. Because the rebuild was <strong>paid-ready from day one</strong> — Google Ads tag, GA4 conversion import, and gclid/UTM attribution all wired in — Apex could launch cleanly.</p>
          <p>Apex Marketings ran a <strong>GCC Google search campaign pointing to a dedicated <code>/get-quote/</code> landing page</strong>, with full conversion tracking. That combination captures high-intent Gulf buyers while the organic rankings mature, and every lead is measured through the same GA4 <code>generate_lead</code> pipeline as the organic and AI-referred traffic.</p>
          <p>The effect is a two-speed engine: paid delivers qualified quote requests now, while SEO and AEO steadily lower the cost of that demand over time.</p>

